  {
    "episode_id": 46698,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46698.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "house and the people who come into there. So absolutely, the quality of life here is fantastic. Well, in 2006, you were elected the US Senate, and you've been serving the US Senate since 2007. And I think one of the, I don't think it'd be unfair to say, and you can tell me if it is, but over that tenure, obviously a lot has happened in our country. And a lot of people will characterize the government as being fairly divided politically over that time. However, I think if anybody were to name people who did their best to work across the aisle and be bipartisan, I think your name would come up in that conversation, right? Like, I think you were part of the Gang of 10, which was a bipartisan initiative. And even the most current legislation which we're going to get into talking about has widespread bipartisan signatures to it. And so, you know, I kind of want to know from you, what is that like? Why have you been able to cultivate that within the US Senate?",
