  {
    "episode_id": 46698,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46698.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "Well, I go back to what you were talking about during the time here as mayor. I mean, you know, we worked with people from all walks of life. And by the way, Chattanooga has been going on an upward trajectory now for 30 years. Think about it. And people have done that in and out. Some of it's been public sector. Some of it's been private sector. But we collectively, as a community, with people of all different kind of backgrounds for 30 years have continued on an upward trajectory and you get to the Senate and it's not like you forget that. It's just how we have operated. I will say that City High School gave me a pretty good education and I can count. And, you know, the fact is that if you're going to pass something in the United States Senate, you need 60 votes. And so, no party has had, well, I guess for a brief period of time, the Democrats had 60 votes by themselves. But generally speaking, in the Senate, you've got to have bipartisan support to make anything big happen. And so, and it's just our nature. I mean, look, I don't want to pass something down partisan lines and then The next group of people come in, the majority changes, and they want to get rid of that bad old thing that happened under only one party control. So look, it's just the way that, again, let's just say this. I think we hopefully have taken a little bit of Chattanooga and Tennessee with us to the United States Senate and understand that the way you get things done over the long haul, things that are going to stand the test of time as you do it, by working with people on both sides of the aisle so that it becomes permanent.",
