  {
    "episode_id": 46698,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46698.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "Oh, you know, I came into the Senate and went on the banking committee after being there a year, and we went through the financial crisis. And, you know, it's a time where you're earning your pay. You know, there were heady times when we really thought the country could collapse and people would be riding on the streets when they couldn't get money out of the ATM. So literally, we thought we were just days away from that. And so I went into a period of time where I was at the center of the universe in many ways, center of what was happening in Washington as a very young senator. And then we went to a phase of about three years where it was just nothing. I mean, it was just absolute. Nothing was happening. And it was And I think that was probably the lowest point where you begin to ask yourself, you know, is it worth a grown man's time to be in the United States Senate with nothing happening.",
