    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46698.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 45,
    "turn_text": "visited many times and Todd has too and listens to their podcasts I know but he passed through with his family and heard a speaker talking about modern slavery. I think you know there's 27 million people enslaved today more than at any time in the world's history. 27 million people to go right now while we're on this podcast. 27 million people are living in slavery. And then he came back through I think about six months later again passing through with his family and goes to a service and the person speaking said talking about who was going to how we're going to deal with this and the person said if not you, who? And so I came to Washington the next week. We travel up separately and share with me what had happened. We had a gentleman come into the office named Gary Haugen who had written a book about it who spends his life trying to end slavery around the world. It came into our office. There were multiple staff follow-ups. We were heading on a trip to Southeast Asia in the fairly near future to deal with some of the trade issues, China issues, South China Sea issues. And we specifically set aside some time to be in the Philippines where we met with about 20 young people who had been enslaved in sexual servitude and sent away to Malaysia and in brothels for years and years and years. A group had, this group, Gary's group actually, was restoring them, had dealt with them through the legal process, and we met with the Philippine, we met with the Manila Police Department and others to understand how they were dealing with it, what was happening from the prosecution standpoint, and I saw First of all, victims, which once you see that, it's difficult to",
